'''ACT New Zealand''' ({{langx|mi|Rōpū ACT}}),<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-05-21 |title=Rōpū ACT - New Zealand Parliament |url=https://www.parliament.nz/mi/mps-and-electorates/political-parties/act-party/ |access-date=2024-05-11 |website=New Zealand Parliament {{!}} Pāremata Aotearoa |language=mi}}</ref> also known as the '''ACT Party''' or simply '''ACT''', is a [[Right-wing politics|right-wing]],{{refn|<ref>{{cite news |last= Sharma |first= Shweta |date= 23 November 2023 |title= New Zealand finally agrees makeup of coalition government after weeks of uncertainty |url= https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/australasia/new-zealand-christopher-luxon-coalation-government-b2452261.html |work= The Independent |access-date= 21 August 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last= Quadri |first= Sami |date= 24 November 2023 |title= New Zealand's National Party forms centre-right coalition after intense negotiations |url= https://www.standard.co.uk/news/world/new-zealand-national-party-christopher-luxon-coalition-government-b1122539.html |work= Evening Standard |access-date= 21 August 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1= Jose |first1= Renju |last2= Craymer |first2= Lucy |date= 23 November 2023 |title= New Zealand's National Party clinches deal to form government |url= https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/new-zealands-national-party-reaches-deal-form-government-2023-11-23/ |work= Reuters |access-date= 21 August 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last= Landsey |first= Amy |date= 16 October 2023 |title= NZ could hold referendum on Treaty of Waitangi despite warnings of 'civil disobedience' over proposed changes to founding document |url= https://www.skynews.com.au/world-news/global-affairs/nz-could-hold-referendum-on-treaty-of-waitangi-despite-warnings-of-civil-disobedience-over-proposed-changes-to-founding-document/news-story/4507e70d43a78db6feca1e3974444b92 |work= Sky News |access-date= 21 August 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last= Chappell |first= Bill |date= 27 November 2023 |title= New Zealand's new government plans to roll back cigarette ban as it funds tax cuts |url= https://www.npr.org/2023/11/27/1215374525/new-zealand-tobacco-cigarette-ban |work= NPR |access-date= 21 August 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last= McKay |first= Ben |date= 5 December 2022 |title= 'Momentous': Ardern issues historic Maori apology and $155m in redress |url= https://www.smh.com.au/world/oceania/momentous-ardern-issues-historic-maori-apology-and-155m-in-redress-20221205-p5c3v0.html |work=[[The Sydney Morning Herald]] |access-date= 21 August 2024}}</ref>}} [[Classical liberalism|classical liberal]],{{refn|<ref name="Boston2003">{{cite book |last1=Boston |first1=Jonathan |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=N-ql-Xs9hhkC&pg=PA92 |title=New Zealand Votes: The General Election of 2002 |date=2003 |publisher=Victoria University Press |isbn=9780864734686 |page=92 |language=en}}</ref><ref name="Seymour2">{{cite speech |last=Seymour |first=David |author-link=David Seymour |title=Our classical liberal tribe |url=http://www.act.org.nz/posts/speech-our-classical-liberal-tribe |website=act.org.nz |publisher=ACT New Zealand |access-date=8 February 2017 |date=23 February 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170211075239/http://www.act.org.nz/posts/speech-our-classical-liberal-tribe |archive-date=11 February 2017 |url-status=dead}}</ref>}} [[Right-libertarianism|right-libertarian]],{{refn|<ref name="policy.nz2">{{cite web |title=Party profile: ACT Party — NZ Election 2020 |url=https://policy.nz/parties/ACT-Party |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201018073035/https://policy.nz/parties/ACT-Party |archive-date=18 October 2020 |access-date=2020-09-25 |website=Your complete guide to NZ Election 2020 – Policy |quote=The ACT Party (Rōpū ACT) is a right-wing libertarian party that advocates free market policies and reducing the role of government.}}</ref><ref name="Graham-McLay-2020"/><ref name="Norris2005">{{cite book |last1=Norris |first1=Pippa |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Gs4BTVg0hXgC&pg=PA285 |title=Radical Right: Voters and Parties in the Electoral Market |date=2005 |publisher=Cambridge University Press |isbn=9781139446426 |page=285 |language=en |access-date=8 February 2017}}</ref>}} and [[Conservatism|conservative]]{{refn|<ref name="Washington Examiner2">{{cite news |last=Perry |first=Nick |date=14 August 2014 |title=Racism mars New Zealand election campaign |url=https://www.washingtonexaminer.com/racism-mars-new-zealand-election-campaign |access-date=13 December 2020 |work=Washington Examiner}}</ref><ref name="News AU2">{{cite news |date=27 November 2011 |title=NZ votes for stability after chaos |url=https://www.news.com.au/world/nz-votes-for-stability-after-chaos/news-story/eed3c5c5e1686793119b3807b15438db |access-date=13 December 2020 |work=News.com.au}}</ref><ref name="The Economist2">{{cite news |date=18 October 2014 |title=Third term lucky |url=https://www.economist.com/banyan/2014/10/18/third-term-lucky |access-date=13 December 2020 |newspaper=[[The Economist]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Smith |first=Ian |date=20 October 2022 |title=Farmers protest 'unworkable regulations' of New Zealand's proposed farm levy |url=https://www.euronews.com/green/2022/10/20/cow-burps-to-be-taxed-under-world-first-proposals-by-new-zealand |access-date=17 April 2024 |work=Euronews}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=24 November 2023 |title=New Zealand's new PM unveils coalition government |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2023/11/24/incoming-new-zealand-pm-unveils-coalition-government_6284566_4.html |access-date=17 April 2024 |work=Le Monde}}</ref>}} [[List of political parties in New Zealand|political party]] in New Zealand. It is currently led by [[David Seymour]].

''ACT'' is an acronym of the name of the '''Association of Consumers and Taxpayers''', a pressure group that was founded in 1993 by former National Party MP [[Derek Quigley]] and former [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] MP [[Roger Douglas]], who served as [[Minister of Finance (New Zealand)|minister of finance]] under the [[Fourth Labour Government of New Zealand|Fourth Labour Government]]. Douglas' [[Neoliberalism|neoliberal]] economic policies, dubbed [[Rogernomics]], transformed [[New Zealand's economy]] from a [[Protectionism|protectionist]] one into a free market through extensive [[deregulation]].

After Labour lost the [[1990 New Zealand general election|1990 election]] and its neoliberal faction lost influence, ACT was built mostly by Douglas' [[Backbone club|former party supporters]] as a new political party for [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]]. The introduction of [[Mixed-member proportional representation|proportional representation]] gave minor parties a greatly increased chance of getting into parliament. Former Labour MP [[Richard Prebble]] won the safe Labour seat of [[Wellington Central (New Zealand electorate)|Wellington Central]] and led ACT to win 6% of the vote in the first MMP election. He continued as ACT party leader from after the election until 2004 when he was succeeded by [[Rodney Hide]]. ACT was briefly led by former [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] leader [[Don Brash]] for the [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011 election]], after which the party [[caucus]] was reduced to one seat. It continued as a single-MP party based out of the [[Epsom (New Zealand electorate)|Epsom]] electorate until [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]].<ref name="2017 general election results">{{cite web |title=2017 General Election – Official Result |url=http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/ |access-date=7 October 2017 |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|New Zealand Electoral Commission]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Explore 2020 New Zealand election results by overall party vote and at each local electorate level. |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |url=https://interactives.stuff.co.nz/2020/10/election-2020-results-live/ |access-date=27 September 2023}}</ref>

ACT supported the [[Fifth National Government of New Zealand|Fifth National Government]] in [[confidence and supply]] from {{NZ election link year|2008}} to 2017. It is currently one of the three parties in the [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand|National-led coalition government]] established in [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]].<ref name="NZ Hearld appointment">{{cite news |date=27 November 2023 |title='Strong, productive, unified': Luxon sworn in as PM |language=en-NZ |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/national-nz-first-act-coalition-christopher-luxon-to-be-sworn-in-as-prime-minister-today-along-with-ministers-new-government-kicks-into-gear/4ACAOUV6RBBOPJTNUJBF3UWYLM/}}</ref><ref name="2023 official results">{{cite web |title=Official count – Overall Results |url=https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/ |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=20 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231107112928/https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/ |archive-date=7 November 2023 |date=3 November 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref>

== History ==
[[File:Roger Douglas 2008.jpg|thumb|upright|Former Minister of Finance Sir Roger Douglas was the party's first leader.]]

=== Formation ===
The name comes from the initials of the Association of Consumers and Taxpayers, founded in 1993 by [[Roger Douglas|Sir Roger Douglas]] and [[Derek Quigley]]. Douglas and Quigley intended the Association to serve as a pressure-group promoting [[Rogernomics]]—the name given to the radical free-market policies implemented by Douglas as [[Minister of Finance (New Zealand)|Minister of Finance]] between 1984 and 1988.<ref>{{cite news |title=Group sets its sights |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=24 August 1993 |page=5 }}</ref> The Association grew out of the '[[Backbone club]]', a [[ginger group]] in the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]] that supported Douglas and his policies.<ref>{{cite news |first=Bernard |last=Orsman |title=Former MPs get Act together |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=24 August 1993 |page=5 }}</ref> In 1996, New Zealand switched to using the [[Mixed-member proportional representation|MMP]] electoral system. The new electoral system gave smaller groups a much better chance of entering Parliament, and encouraged the Association to transform into a political party and contest elections.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Curtin |first1=Jennifer |last2=Miller |first2=Raymond |title=Political parties – Small parties under MMP |url= http://www.teara.govt.nz/en/photograph/34331/act-leader-and-founder-2008#comment-154151 |publisher=[[Te Ara: The Encyclopedia of New Zealand]] |access-date=26 July 2013 |date=16 November 2012}}</ref> The nascent party's manifesto was based upon a book written by Douglas entitled ''Unfinished Business''. Douglas served as ACT's first leader, but soon stood aside for [[Richard Prebble]] (his old ally from their days in the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]).<ref>{{cite book |last1=Roper |first1=Juliet |last2=Holtz-Bacha |first2=Christina |last3=Mazzoleni |first3=Gianpietro |title=The Politics of Representation: Election Campaigning and Proportional Representation |publisher=Peter Lang |date=2004 |isbn=978-0-820-46148-9|page=35}}</ref>

=== 1996–2004: Prebble's leadership ===
[[File:Richard Prebble (cropped) 1993.jpg|thumb|upright|left|Richard Prebble led ACT from 1996 to 2004.]]
Under Douglas, ACT had languished at 1% in opinion polls, but with Prebble's [[populist]] rhetoric the party increased in support.<ref name="Trotter">{{cite news |last1=Trotter |first1=Chris |title=Chris Trotter argues Richard Prebble made Act competitive electorally by turning it into a right-wing populist party, and asks whether David Seymour is doing the same |url=https://www.interest.co.nz/opinion/106326/chris-trotter-argues-richard-prebble-made-act-competitive-electorally-turning-it |access-date=10 January 2021 |work=interest.co.nz |date=2 August 2020}}</ref> In the {{NZ election link|1996}}, ACT fielded 56 [[party list|list candidates]].<ref name="1996 successful">{{cite web|title= Part III – Party Lists of Successful Registered Parties |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_1996/pdf/3.3%20Party%20Lists%20of%20Successful%20Registered%20Parties.pdf |publisher=Electoral Commission |access-date= 27 July 2013 |url-status= dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20130208054410/http://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_1996/pdf/3.3%20Party%20Lists%20of%20Successful%20Registered%20Parties.pdf |archive-date= 8 February 2013 }}</ref> Prebble won the{{NZ electorate link| Wellington Central}} electorate,<ref name="1996 successful" /> and with 6.10% of the total party vote, ACT also sent seven [[list MP]]s to the [[45th New Zealand Parliament]].<ref name=":0">{{cite web |url= http://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_1996/pdf/1.1%20Summary%20of%20overall%20results.pdf |title= Part I: Summary of Party List and Electorate Candidate Seats |publisher= New Zealand Chief Electoral Office |year= 2007 |access-date= 27 July 2013 |archive-date= 22 May 2010 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100522105133/http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_1996/pdf/1.1%20Summary%20of%20overall%20results.pdf |url-status= dead }}</ref>

In the {{NZ election link|1999}}, ACT obtained 7.04% of the party vote, making it eligible for nine list MPs.<ref name=":1">{{cite web | url=http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_1999/e9/html/e9_partI.html | title=Election Results 1999: Summary of Overall Results | work=Electionresults.govt.nz | publisher=New Zealand Electoral Commission | access-date=2 May 2014}}</ref>

In the {{NZ election link|2002}}, ACT obtained 7.14% of the party vote, making it eligible for nine list MPs.<ref name="2002 election">{{cite web |title=Official Count Results – Overall Status |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2002/partystatus.html |publisher=Electoral Commission |access-date=7 August 2013}}</ref>

On 2 December 2004, both Douglas and Quigley announced that they would step down as patrons of ACT. They stated as the reason that they wished to have more freedom to disagree with the party publicly.<ref>{{cite news|title=Sir Roger Douglas|url=http://www.magazinestoday.co.nz/sir-roger-douglas/|access-date=20 June 2017|work=Magazines Today|date=10 January 2016|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180204161944/http://www.magazinestoday.co.nz/sir-roger-douglas/|archive-date=4 February 2018}}</ref>

=== 2005 election ===
Prebble's sudden departure from the leadership of ACT in 2004 signalled a decline in the party's electoral fortunes.<ref name="Trotter"/> [[Rodney Hide]] led ACT into the {{NZ election link|2005}}. It obtained 1.51% of the party vote, but due to Hide winning the seat of [[Epsom (New Zealand electorate)|Epsom]], it did not need to obtain the necessary 5% threshold of the party vote. This was only enough to allow one list MP [[Heather Roy]], to join Hide in parliament.<ref name=":2">{{cite web | url=http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2005/partystatus.html | title=Election Results 2005: Official Count Results – Overall Status | work=Electionresults.govt.nz | publisher=New Zealand Electoral Commission | access-date=2 May 2014}}</ref>

=== 2008–2011: First term in government ===
In the [[2008 New Zealand general election]], ACT fielded 61 [[Party lists in the 2008 New Zealand general election|list candidates]], starting with [[Rodney Hide]], [[Heather Roy]], Sir [[Roger Douglas]], [[John Boscawen]], [[David Garrett (politician)|David Garrett]] and [[Hilary Calvert]]. The election marked an improvement in ACT's fortunes. Hide retained his Epsom seat and ACT's share of the party vote increased to 3.65% (up from the 1.5% gained in the 2005 election). The combination allowed the party five MPs in total.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2008/partystatus.html | title=Chief Electoral Office: Official Count results: Overall status.}}</ref>

In addition, the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] won the most seats overall, forming a [[minority government]], the [[Fifth National Government of New Zealand]], with the support of ACT as well as the [[Māori Party]] and [[United Future]]. John Key offered both Hide and Roy posts as Ministers outside [[Fifth National Government of New Zealand|Cabinet]]: Hide became Minister of Local Government, Minister for Regulatory Reform and Associate Minister of Commerce, while Roy became Minister of Consumer Affairs, Associate [[Minister of Defence (New Zealand)|Minister of Defence]] and Associate [[Minister of Education (New Zealand)|Minister of Education]].<ref name="NZ_Herald_10543509">{{cite news |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10543509 |title=Key's Government |date=17 November 2008 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=15 September 2011}}</ref>

After 2008, some caucus MPs and organisational members became dissatisfied with ACT's coalition partner status and argued at ACT's national conference (27 February 2010) that there were insufficient fiscal responsibility policy gains for their party and that the National Party had slid from its earlier commitment to the politics of fiscal responsibility over the course of the previous decade. Throughout 2009, there had been at least one reported ACT caucus coup attempt against Hide's leadership, believed to have been led by Deputy Leader Heather Roy and Roger Douglas. However, it faltered when Prime Minister Key supported Hide's retention and threatened a snap election. In addition, the party's polling of a lowly one to two percent in most opinion polls meant only Heather Roy might accompany Hide after any forthcoming general election, if Hide retained ACT's Epsom pivotal electorate seat.<ref>{{Cite news|first= Martin |last= Kay |title=Leader warns ACT's hardliners |work= Dominion Post |date=27 February 2010 |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/3382781/Leader-warns-Acts-hardliners}}</ref>

On 28 April 2011, Hide announced that he was resigning the ACT leadership in favour of former National Party leader and [[Reserve Bank of New Zealand|Reserve Bank]] Governor [[Don Brash]] who joined the party that morning. Brash's leadership was unanimously approved by the party board and parliamentary caucus on 30 April.<ref>{{cite news |title=Act accepts Brash as leader |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=10722553 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=30 April 2011 |access-date=30 April 2011}}</ref> Brash promised to focus the party on controlling [[government debt]], equality between Māori and non-Māori, and rethinking the [[Emissions Trading Scheme]], with a target of getting 15 percent of the party vote in the next election.<ref name="Coup aside, bold Brash">{{cite news |title=Coup aside, bold Brash must deliver on gamble |first=Fran |last=O'Sullivan |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/opinion/news/article.cfm?c_id=466&objectid=10722361 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=30 April 2011 |access-date=30 April 2011}}</ref>

In November 2011, a recording of a conversation held between [[John Key]] and the former National Party member and former [[Mayor of Auckland City]] [[John Banks (New Zealand politician)|John Banks]], who had been selected as the new ACT candidate in Epsom, was leaked to [[The New Zealand Herald#Herald on Sunday|''Herald on Sunday'']].<ref name="3 News">{{cite news |url=http://www.3news.co.nz/Key-may-face-more-teapot-tape-accusations/tabid/419/articleID/232874/Default.aspx |title=Key may face more teapot tape accusations |author=Wilson, Peter |date=16 November 2011 |work=[[3 News]] |location=New Zealand |access-date=16 November 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120201110834/http://www.3news.co.nz/Key-may-face-more-teapot-tape-accusations/tabid/419/articleID/232874/Default.aspx |archive-date=1 February 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=10766303 |title='Teapot tape' could nail lid to ACT coffin |author=Armstrong, John |date=16 November 2011 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |location=New Zealand  |access-date=16 November 2011}}</ref> [[3 News]] also obtained copies of the recording suggesting the two politicians were discussing issues related to ACT New Zealand's leadership.<ref name="3 News"/> Media dubbed the affair [[tea tape scandal|teapot tape]].<ref name="3 News"/>

=== 2011 election: Decline ===
In the [[2011 New Zealand general election]], ACT fielded 55 [[Party lists in the 2011 New Zealand general election|list candidates]], starting with new leader [[Don Brash]], [[Catherine Isaac]], Don Nicolson, [[John Banks (New Zealand politician)|John Banks]], [[David Seymour]] and Chris Simmons.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.act.org.nz/candidates |title=Candidates &#124; ACT New Zealand |access-date=20 August 2008 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111014041050/http://www.act.org.nz/candidates |archive-date=14 October 2011 }}</ref> The election was a disappointment for ACT, with the party's worst election result since it began in 1996. John Banks retained the Epsom seat for ACT, however the 34.2% majority held by Rodney Hide was severely cut back to 6.3% as large numbers of Labour and Green voters in Epsom tactically split their vote and gave their electorate vote to the National candidate Paul Goldsmith. Nationwide, ACT received only 1.07% of the party vote, placing eighth out of 13 on party vote percentage.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://electionresults.org.nz/electionresults_2011/|title=Chief Electoral Office: Official Count results: Overall status.|access-date=27 November 2011|archive-date=26 February 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120226180844/http://www.electionresults.org.nz/electionresults_2011/|url-status=dead}}</ref> As a result, ACT were only entitled to one seat in the new Parliament, filled by John Banks. Subsequently, Don Brash announced that he had stepped down as leader during his speech on election night.<ref>{{cite news |title=Don Brash interview announcing resignation as ACT party leader|url=http://www.radionz.co.nz/audio/player/2503502|date=26 November 2011 |access-date=27 November 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=John Banks' comments on Don Brash's resignation|url=http://tvnz.co.nz/election-2011/brash-s-resignation-gracious-thing-do-banks-4573771 |date=27 November 2011 |access-date=27 November 2011}}</ref> Following the 2011 general election John Banks stated that he believed that the ACT brand "...just about had its use-by date..." and needed to be renamed and relaunched.<ref>{{cite news |title=John Banks' comments on ACT Party brand|url=http://www.radionz.co.nz/audio/player/2503645|date=28 November 2011 |access-date=29 November 2011}}</ref>

Their previous partners, the National Party, again won the most seats overall, and formed a [[minority government]]. The [[Fifth National Government of New Zealand]] had ACT support as well as that of [[United Future]] and the [[Māori Party]], providing the coalition with [[confidence and supply]].

=== 2014 election ===
[[File:ACT Selection Announcement for Leader and Epsom- Seymour & Whyte.jpg|thumb|David Seymour and Jamie Whyte in February 2014]]
ACT leader John Banks announced in December of 2013 that he would retire at the next election and stand down as party leader, to focus on contesting allegations of electoral fraud in court.<ref>{{cite news |title=Banks to quit politics and fight court case |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/229757/banks-to-quit-politics-and-fight-court-case |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=4 December 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916041312/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/229757/banks-to-quit-politics-and-fight-court-case |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Three candidates declared their intention to replace Banks: John Boscawen and [[Jamie Whyte]] stood to replace him both as ACT leader and Epsom candidate, while David Seymour only stood for the latter role.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT candidates hold public meeting |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/234756/act-candidates-hold-public-meeting |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=30 January 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916041847/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/234756/act-candidates-hold-public-meeting |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> In February 2014, the ACT board announced they had selected Whyte as the party's leader and Seymour as their candidate for Epsom.<ref name="Young-2014">{{cite news |last1=Young |first1=Audrey |title=Jamie Whyte elected Act leader |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/jamie-whyte-elected-act-leader/X5FYGX5BX6US3SV3RB2JE2HWZA/ |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=2 February 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916042036/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/jamie-whyte-elected-act-leader/X5FYGX5BX6US3SV3RB2JE2HWZA/ |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Whyte, who had only recently returned to the country, had a degree in philosophy and was a noted writer and columnist in Britain.<ref name="Young-2014" /><ref name="Young-2014a">{{cite news |last1=Young |first1=Audrey |title=Act leader set to play it straight |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/act-leader-set-to-play-it-straight/ONXVZQ2Z22FJL4JFOECJAW473Y/ |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=3 February 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916042110/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/act-leader-set-to-play-it-straight/ONXVZQ2Z22FJL4JFOECJAW473Y/ |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Seymour first stood for the party in 2005, and was employed by a Canadian think tank at the time of his selection. Boscawen had strongly opposed selecting separate candidates for the leadership and Epsom, and resigned as party president after the announcement.<ref name="Young-2014" />

Later in the month, Whyte stated to the blog ''The Ruminator'' that he did not believe consensual [[incestuous]] relationships should be illegal.<ref name="NZ Herald-2014">{{cite news |title=Act Leader Jamie Whyte stands by incest comments |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/act-leader-jamie-whyte-stands-by-incest-comments/IWFUOHB7LNIDQ7GSW6DFVJRDO4/ |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=26 February 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916045735/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/act-leader-jamie-whyte-stands-by-incest-comments/IWFUOHB7LNIDQ7GSW6DFVJRDO4/ |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Batt |first1=Tim |title=Mr Ryght: An interview with ACT leader: Jamie Whyte |url=https://ruminator.co.nz/mr-ryght/ |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=The Ruminator |date=26 February 2014 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/btcJs |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Whyte initially defended his statement, which he said did not reflect party policy.<ref name="NZ Herald-2014" /> However, Whyte said the following day that he had regretted the comments.<ref>{{cite news |title=Act leader 'regrets' incest comments |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/act-leader-regrets-incest-comments/HQPMXLFYUB4O2IW4Z6X37R6SCM/ |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=27 February 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916045940/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/act-leader-regrets-incest-comments/HQPMXLFYUB4O2IW4Z6X37R6SCM/ |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Act leader regrets incest comment |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/237446/act-leader-regrets-incest-comment |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=27 February 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916051143/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/237446/act-leader-regrets-incest-comment |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>
[[File:ACT New Zealand logo, 2017 variant.svg|thumb|upright=0.5|left|Party logo adopted in 2014]]
At the party's conference in March, Whyte announced ACT's support for a [[three strikes (policy)|three strikes policy]] for burglary, and for reviewing and replacing the [[Resource Management Act 1991|Resource Management Act]]. A new yellow logo was also introduced for the party.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Vance |first1=Andrea |title=ACT's new man Jamie Whyte speaks up |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/9779271/ACTs-new-man-Jamie-Whyte-speaks-up |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=1 March 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916051714/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/9779271/ACTs-new-man-Jamie-Whyte-speaks-up |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Kenneth Wang (politician)|Kenneth Wang]], previously a list MP for ACT, was elected as the deputy leader by the party board in April.<ref>{{cite news |title=Kenneth Wang new ACT deputy leader |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/241738/kenneth-wang-new-act-deputy-leader |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=15 April 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916051820/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/241738/kenneth-wang-new-act-deputy-leader |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Whyte said in July that he would stand in the [[Pakuranga (electorate)|Pakuranga electorate]], although he would only campaign for the party vote.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT leader to stand in Pakuranga |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/250272/act-leader-to-stand-in-pakuranga |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=21 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916051916/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/250272/act-leader-to-stand-in-pakuranga |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In late July, Whyte called for the abolition of laws based on race, saying that Māori enjoyed legal privilege akin to that of the aristocracy in pre-revolutionary France.<ref>{{cite news |title=Act's Whyte calls for end to race based laws |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/acts-whyte-calls-for-end-to-race-based-laws/GKVASC4X4DCROHV4FZRIHNKAZI/ |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=29 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916054430/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/acts-whyte-calls-for-end-to-race-based-laws/GKVASC4X4DCROHV4FZRIHNKAZI/ |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Susan Devoy]], the [[Race Relations Commissioner]], described Whyte's statement as "grotesque and inflammatory".<ref>{{cite news |title=Devoy slams ACT for Maori attacks |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/10329249/Devoy-slams-ACT-for-Maori-attacks |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=30 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916064328/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/10329249/Devoy-slams-ACT-for-Maori-attacks |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Whyte called for her resignation in response, describing Devoy's comment as electoral interference, and for the abolition of the Race Relations Commissioner.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fuatai |first1=Teuila |title=Act leader Jamie Whyte caught short again |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/kahu/act-leader-jamie-whyte-caught-short-again/NO454NNKJZPKXFZXGMTMPCELZM/ |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=5 August 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916064426/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/kahu/act-leader-jamie-whyte-caught-short-again/NO454NNKJZPKXFZXGMTMPCELZM/ |archive-date=16 September 2026}}</ref> He named Fiji's removal of race-based laws as a prospective model for New Zealand.<ref name="NZ Herald-2014a">{{cite news |title=Act's Whyte suggests Fiji as model for ending NZ race laws |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/acts-whyte-suggests-fiji-as-model-for-ending-nz-race-laws/6MYLNU4U4CGYEW2FPEJB77U2UU/ |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=7 August 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916064441/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/acts-whyte-suggests-fiji-as-model-for-ending-nz-race-laws/6MYLNU4U4CGYEW2FPEJB77U2UU/ |archive-date=16 September 2026}}</ref> Whyte's comments on race relations caused Guy McCallum, an ACT board member and candidate in [[Dunedin North (electorate)|Dunedin North]], to resign from the party.<ref name="NZ Herald-2014a" /><ref>{{cite news |title=ACT candidate protests, quits party |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-ao-maori/251537/act-candidate-protests%2C-quits-party |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=6 August 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916065616/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/te-ao-maori/251537/act-candidate-protests,-quits-party |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

At the [[2014 New Zealand general election|2014 election]], Seymour won the Epsom electorate, but ACT's party vote of 0.69%<ref name="RNZ-2014">{{cite news |title=Jamie Whyte resigns as leader of ACT |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/256111/jamie-whyte-resigns-as-leader-of-act |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=3 October 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916075332/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/256111/jamie-whyte-resigns-as-leader-of-act |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> was not high enough to elect a list MP.<ref>{{cite news |title=National's potential partners win seats |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/255125/national%27s-potential-partners-win-seats |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=20 September 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916070613/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/255125/national%27s-potential-partners-win-seats |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Kenny |first1=Katie |last2=Mann |first2=Brittany |title=Mayors leave chains behind |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/manawatu-standard/news/10526719/Mayors-leave-chains-behind |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Manawatū Standard]] |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=21 September 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916070614/https://www.stuff.co.nz/manawatu-standard/news/10526719/Mayors-leave-chains-behind |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>  National Party leader and prime minister John Key had encouraged his party's supporters in Epsom to vote for Seymour.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Fox |first1=Michael |last2=Walters |first2=Laura |title=National to stick with ACT, UnitedFuture and Maori |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/10320342/National-to-stick-with-ACT-UnitedFuture-and-Maori |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=28 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916073619/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/10320342/National-to-stick-with-ACT-UnitedFuture-and-Maori |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Deals show contempt, says Labour |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/250844/deals-show-contempt,-says-labour |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=29 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916074343/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/250844/deals-show-contempt,-says-labour |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Trevett |first1=Claire |title=Labour 'distracted' by dirty politics - Key |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/labour-distracted-by-dirty-politics-key/UGFOPF3UEOJVHSAFGYKP6BXF2U/ |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=6 September 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916074657/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/labour-distracted-by-dirty-politics-key/UGFOPF3UEOJVHSAFGYKP6BXF2U/ |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Most National party voters in Epsom followed his recommendation and split their vote, with 60 per cent giving their candidate vote to Seymour.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Davison |first1=Isaac |title=Nats' strategic voting vital for Act |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/nats-strategic-voting-vital-for-act/6VWFSPR6MJVQ6DYDOYJ74LSLSA/ |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=20 October 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916074802/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/nats-strategic-voting-vital-for-act/6VWFSPR6MJVQ6DYDOYJ74LSLSA/ |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

ACT agreed to support the [[Fifth National Government of New Zealand|National government]] for a third term, with Seymour being named [[parliamentary under-secretary]] to the education and regulatory reform ministers.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Davison |first1=Isaac |title=Act deal: No portfolio for David Seymour |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/act-deal-no-portfolio-for-david-seymour/HTEGOHCFLZRIOYFHF656BX6CKY/ |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=29 September 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916071258/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/act-deal-no-portfolio-for-david-seymour/HTEGOHCFLZRIOYFHF656BX6CKY/ |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=UF, ACT sign up to support National |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/255729/uf%2C-act-sign-up-to-support-national |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=29 September 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916071230/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/255729/uf,-act-sign-up-to-support-national |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Whyte resigned as party leader on 3 October, and was replaced by Seymour.<ref name="RNZ-2014" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Vance |first1=Andrea |title=ACT's Jamie Whyte quits as leader |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/10576531/ACTs-Jamie-Whyte-quits-as-leader |access-date=16 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=3 October 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260916075104/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/10576531/ACTs-Jamie-Whyte-quits-as-leader |archive-date=16 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== 2017&ndash;2020 term: resurgence===
Wang resigned as deputy leader on 9 July 2017, the same day ACT released its party list; Beth Houlbrooke was announced as his replacement.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/94542326/act-party-list-prompts-resignation-of-deputy-leader-kenneth-wang|title=ACT party list prompts resignation of deputy leader Kenneth Wang |last=Small |first=Vernon |date=9 July 2017 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |access-date=9 July 2017 }}</ref>
{{Multiple image
 | width = 150
 | image1 = Logo der Freien Demokraten.svg
 | class1 = bg-transparent
 | alt1 = FDP logo
 | image2 = ACT New Zealand CMYK.svg
 | class2 = bg-transparent
 | alt2 = ACT logo
 | footer = The Free Democratic Party logo adopted after 2013, and ACT's new logo adopted in 2019.
}}
The party list had 39 candidates, none of whom were elected.<ref>{{cite web|title=2017 GENERAL ELECTION PARTY LISTS|url=http://www.elections.org.nz/events/2017-general-election/2017-general-election-party-lists|website=Electoral Commission|access-date=12 September 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180420193402/http://www.elections.org.nz/events/2017-general-election/2017-general-election-party-lists|archive-date=20 April 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref> While Seymour retained the Epsom electorate, entitling him to a seat in parliament, ACT received 0.5% of the party vote, its worst result to date.<ref name="2017 general election results" /><ref name="Manhire-2023">{{cite news |last1=Manhire |first1=Toby |author1-link=Toby Manhire |title=The fall and rise of David Seymour and the Act Party |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/15-06-2023/act-ascendent-the-story-behind-the-seymour-led-rise-and-refusal-to-fall |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[The Spinoff]] |date=15 June 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260915100021/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/15-06-2023/act-ascendent-the-story-behind-the-seymour-led-rise-and-refusal-to-fall |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Houlbrooke |first1=Louis |title=My year as an ACT supporter |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2017/12/27/my-year-as-an-act-supporter/ |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=27 December 2017 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/z8Wap |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Following the 2017 election, Seymour considered rebranding the party to the Liberals.<ref name="Manhire-2023" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Sachdeva |first1=Sam |title=ACT's battle for significance |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2017/10/03/acts-battle-for-significance/ |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=3 October 2017 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/KICOS |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Amidst this, Seymour visited Berlin in 2018 as a guest of the [[Friedrich Naumann Foundation]], which is affiliated to the [[Free Democratic Party]] (FDP).<ref>{{cite news |last1=Seymour |first1=David |author1-link=David Seymour |title=David Seymour: The real story of our surge from one MP to 10 at the 2020 election |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/books/12-11-2021/david-seymour-the-real-story-of-our-surge-from-one-mp-to-10-at-the-2020-election |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[The Spinoff]] |date=12 November 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260915114318/https://thespinoff.co.nz/books/12-11-2021/david-seymour-the-real-story-of-our-surge-from-one-mp-to-10-at-the-2020-election |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Ideologically similar to ACT, the FDP had lost its seats in Germany's [[Bundestag]] in [[2013 German federal election|2013]], but returned at the [[2017 German federal election|2017 election]] after a rebrand. In June 2019,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Devlin |first1=Collette |title=More colour, no surprises in ACT Party re-brand but 'hairy goal' set of 14 MPs at next election |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/113521522/more-colour-no-surprises-in-act-party-rebrand-but-hairy-goal-set-of-14-mps-at-next-election |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=16 June 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260915220043/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/113521522/more-colour-no-surprises-in-act-party-rebrand-but-hairy-goal-set-of-14-mps-at-next-election |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Jennings |first1=Mark |title=Act goes pink for party revamp |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2019/06/16/act-goes-pink-for-party-revamp/ |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=16 June 2019 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/Fada2 |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> ACT changed its party logo to closely resemble the FDP's, with the addition of the colour magenta.<ref name="Manhire-2023" /><ref name="Vance-2020">{{cite news |last1=Vance |first1=Andrea |title=Election 2020: The strategic error that cost Winston Peters - and boosted a determined, wily David Seymour |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300140171/election-2020-the-strategic-error-that-cost-winston-peters--and-boosted-a-determined-wily-david-seymour |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=26 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260915100018/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300140171/election-2020-the-strategic-error-that-cost-winston-peters--and-boosted-a-determined-wily-david-seymour |archive-date=15 September 2026}}</ref>

As the party's political prospects languished, Seymour accepted an invitation to appear on the television show ''[[Dancing with the Stars (New Zealand TV series)|Dancing with the Stars]]'' in late 2017.<ref name="Manhire-2023" /> His performance on the show, where he [[twerk]]ed in neon [[lycra]], was described by [[Toby Manhire]] of ''[[The Spinoff]]'' as "hapless" but ultimately successful in raising Seymour's public profile.<ref name="Graham-McLay-2020">{{cite news |last1=Graham-McLay |first1=Charlotte |title=ACT's David Seymour: 'I don’t really care what people think and I’m still quite successful' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/aug/08/acts-david-seymour-i-dont-really-care-what-people-think-and-im-still-quite-successful |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=7 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260915095807/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2020/aug/08/acts-david-seymour-i-dont-really-care-what-people-think-and-im-still-quite-successful |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Manhire-2023" /> Following the [[Christchurch mosque shootings]], Seymour was the only member of parliament to oppose [[Arms (Prohibited Firearms, Magazines, and Parts) Amendment Act 2019|a ban on semi-automatic rifles]].<ref name="Jennings-2020">{{cite news |last1=Jennings |first1=Mark |title=David Seymour: How did he do it? |url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2020/10/17/david-seymour-how-did-he-do-it/ |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=17 October 2020 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/YO4XW |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Duncan-2020">{{cite news |last1=Duncan |first1=Grant |title=The rise of ACT in 2020 highlights tensions between the party’s libertarian and populist traditions |url=https://theconversation.com/the-rise-of-act-in-2020-highlights-tensions-between-the-partys-libertarian-and-populist-traditions-147170 |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[The Conversation (website)|The Conversation]] |date=6 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260915095753/https://theconversation.com/the-rise-of-act-in-2020-highlights-tensions-between-the-partys-libertarian-and-populist-traditions-147170 |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> ACT's support among the gun-owning community of New Zealand rose as a result, with firearms advocate [[Nicole McKee]] joining the party.<ref name="Jennings-2020" /><ref name="Sowman-Lund-2020">{{cite news |last1=Sowman-Lund |first1=Stewart |title=Davidmania? The Spinoff meets Act’s high-flying leader |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/03-10-2020/davidmania-the-spinoff-meets-acts-high-flying-leader |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[The Spinoff]] |date=3 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260915095831/https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/03-10-2020/davidmania-the-spinoff-meets-acts-high-flying-leader |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Andrea Vance of ''[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]]'' described the [[New Zealand First|NZ First]] party's support of the firearm reforms as a strategic error, which allowed for ACT to gain support.<ref name="Vance-2020" /> Both Manhire and Grant Duncan in ''[[The Conversation (website)|The Conversation]]'' state that ACT's rise in support came in part from NZ First supporters dissatisfied with the Labour government.<ref name="Manhire-2023" /><ref name="Duncan-2020" />

Seymour had also introduced the [[End of Life Choice Bill]] to parliament, which would allow for [[voluntary euthanasia]].<ref name="Vance-2020" /><ref name="Duncan-2020" /> [[Brooke van Velden]], then a staffer and later ACT's deputy leader, helped to promote the bill.<ref name="Manhire-2023" /> The bill passed in November 2019, was approved in a [[2020 New Zealand euthanasia referendum|2020 referendum]], and came into effect in late 2021.<ref name="Vance-2020" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Martin |first1=Hannah |title=Assisted dying now legal in New Zealand, End of Life Choice Act a 'huge relief' for some |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/health/euthanasia-debate/300443715/assisted-dying-now-legal-in-new-zealand-end-of-life-choice-act-a-huge-relief-for-some |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=7 November 2021 |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/7fcVc |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

By the start of 2020, ACT's support in opinion polls still remained negligible.<ref name="Jennings-2020" /><ref name="Sowman-Lund-2020" /> However, Seymour was appointed as a member of the [[Epidemic Response Committee]] as the [[COVID-19 pandemic]] began.<ref name="Manhire-2023" /><ref name="Jennings-2020" /> Seymour was a sharp critic of the government's handling of the pandemic, in contrast to a more muddled stance from the National Party.<ref name="Vance-2020" /><ref name="Jennings-2020" /> ACT supported the reopening of international borders, and cited Taiwan, which had adopted extensive [[contact tracing]], as an example to follow for New Zealand. While he did not support a [[herd immunity]] response to the pandemic, Seymour was sceptical of the efficacy of lockdowns.<ref name="Van Veen & Oldfield-2024">{{cite book |last1=Van Veen |first1=Josh |last2=Oldfield |first2=Luke |editor1-last=Curtin |editor1-first=Jennifer |editor2-last=Greaves |editor2-first=Lara |editor3-last=Vowles |editor3-first=Jack |editor1-link=Jennifer Curtin |chapter=Boycotting the ‘Team of Five Million’ |date=2024 |publisher=[[ANU Press]] |title=A Team of Five Million?: The 2020 'Covid-19' New Zealand General Election |isbn=978-1-76046-648-0 |pages=222, 231 |chapter-url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/jj.17286118.15}}</ref> Meanwhile, National faced internal strife, having replaced [[Simon Bridges|its leader]] after his criticisms of COVID-19 policy, electing [[Todd Muller]] as leader, who would resign after 57 days, leading to [[Judith Collins]]' elevation.<ref name="Manhire-2023" /><ref name="Van Veen & Oldfield-2024" /> By August, a [[Colmar Brunton]] poll had shown ACT at close to 5% support, which would see the party elect six MPs.<ref name="Graham-McLay-2020"/> In advance of the election in October, ACT was at 8% support in polls.<ref name="Duncan-2020" /><ref name="Sowman-Lund-2020" />

The party's list for the 2020 election was announced on 28 June. Van Velden was announced as the party's new deputy and placed in the second spot on the list, while McKee was placed third.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cooke |first1=Henry |title=ACT reveals new list, elects 27-year-old staffer deputy leader and puts gun lobbyist at number three |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300044090/act-reveals-new-list-elects-27yearold-staffer-deputy-leader-and-puts-gun-lobbyist-at-number-three |access-date=15 September 2026 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=28 June 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260915140512/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300044090/act-reveals-new-list-elects-27yearold-staffer-deputy-leader-and-puts-gun-lobbyist-at-number-three |archive-date=15 September 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== 2020&ndash;2023 term ===
Following the election, held on 17 October (postponed from September), ACT increased their share of the party vote to 7.6%, winning 10 seats including Seymour's Epsom seat and nine from the party list.<ref>{{Cite news|date=2020-10-17|title=Election 2020: The night in pictures as voting results roll in|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300135312/election-2020-the-night-in-pictures-as-voting-results-roll-in|access-date=2020-10-17|work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]]|language=en|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201018082423/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300135312/election-2020-the-night-in-pictures-as-voting-results-roll-in|archive-date=18 October 2020}}</ref><ref name="results2020">{{cite web|date=2020-11-06|title=2020 General Election and Referendums – Official Result|url=https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230621001306/https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/|url-status=dead|archive-date=21 June 2023|access-date=2020-11-06|website=Electoral Commission}}</ref> This was the party's best-ever result.<ref>{{cite news |title=Election 2020: Act's voters 'came home' but David Seymour's challenge now is to keep them there |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/election-2020-acts-voters-came-home-but-david-seymours-challenge-now-is-to-keep-them-there/OQKMT6P7UBAXVSP7U6QQPG7ZIY/ |access-date=11 January 2021 |date=18 October 2020 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ}}</ref>

In late April 2021, the ACT party sponsored motion asking the New Zealand Parliament to debate and vote on the issue of human rights abuses against the [[Uyghurs|Uyghur]] ethnic minority community in [[China]]'s [[Xinjiang]] province.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Manch |first1=Thomas |title=ACT party will file motion asking Parliament to debate Xinjiang 'genocide' |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/124955298/act-party-will-file-motion-asking-parliament-to-debate-xinjiang-genocide |access-date=29 April 2021 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=28 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210428002944/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/124955298/act-party-will-file-motion-asking-parliament-to-debate-xinjiang-genocide |archive-date=28 April 2021|url-status=live}}</ref> In early May, the incumbent Labour Party revised the motion to raise concerns about human rights abuses in Xinjiang but omitting the term genocide, which was subsequently adopted by the New Zealand Parliament on 5 May.<ref>{{cite news |last1=McClure |first1=Tess |title=New Zealand draws back from calling Chinese abuses of Uyghurs genocide |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/04/new-zealand-draws-back-from-calling-chinese-abuses-of-uyghurs-genocide |access-date=5 May 2021 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=4 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210504193229/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/04/new-zealand-draws-back-from-calling-chinese-abuses-of-uyghurs-genocide |archive-date=4 May 2021|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Manch |first1=Thomas |title=Parliament unanimously declares 'severe human rights abuses' occurring against Uyghur in China |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/125034356/parliament-unanimously-declares-severe-human-rights-abuses-occurring-against-uyghur-in-china |access-date=5 May 2021 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=5 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210505052434/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/125034356/parliament-unanimously-declares-severe-human-rights-abuses-occurring-against-uyghur-in-china|archive-date=5 May 2021|url-status=live}}</ref> In response, the Chinese Embassy claimed that the motion made "groundless accusations" of human rights abuses against China and constituted an interference in China's internal affairs.<ref>{{cite web |title=Response to Media Query by Spokesperson of Chinese Embassy in New Zealand about Motion on Xinjiang Passed by the NZ Parliament |url=http://www.chinaembassy.org.nz/eng/zxgxs/t1873530.htm |publisher=Embassy of the People's Republic of China in New Zealand |access-date=7 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210506080537/http://www.chinaembassy.org.nz/eng/zxgxs/t1873530.htm |archive-date=6 May 2021 |date=5 May 2021 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Neilson |first1=Michael |title=China lashes out after New Zealand Parliament makes Uighur declaration over 'severe human rights abuses' |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/china-lashes-out-after-new-zealand-parliament-makes-uighur-declaration-over-severe-human-rights-abuses/NMSWDVCV7MNDEI35KO4YGXQVK4/ |access-date=7 May 2021 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=6 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210506131749/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/china-lashes-out-after-new-zealand-parliament-makes-uighur-declaration-over-severe-human-rights-abuses/NMSWDVCV7MNDEI35KO4YGXQVK4/ |archive-date=6 May 2021|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 19 May 2021, the ACT Party opposed [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] MP [[Golriz Ghahraman]]'s motion calling for members of parliament to recognise the rights of Palestinians to self-determination and statehood while reaffirming its support for a two-state solution to the [[Israel-Palestine conflict]]. Deputy Leader Van Velden justified ACT's opposition to the Green motion on the basis of Green MP [[Ricardo Menéndez March]]'s tweet that said "From the river to the sea, Palestine will be free!."<ref>{{cite news |title= Green Party motion to call for recognition of Palestine's right to self-determination |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/442887/green-party-motion-to-call-for-recognition-of-palestine-s-right-to-self-determination |access-date=21 May 2021 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=19 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210520003402/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/442887/green-party-motion-to-call-for-recognition-of-palestine-s-right-to-self-determination |archive-date=20 May 2021|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Manch |first1=Thomas |title=Green Party's motion to declare Palestine a state fails in Parliament |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/125175638/green-partys-motion-to-declare-palestine-a-state-fails-in-parliament |access-date=21 May 2021 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=19 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210520053549/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/125175638/green-partys-motion-to-declare-palestine-a-state-fails-in-parliament |archive-date=20 May 2021|url-status=live}}</ref>

===2023&ndash;present: Coalition with National===
{{Main|Sixth National Government of New Zealand}}

Final results of the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]] indicated that ACT won 8.64% of the party vote, resulting in 11 seats.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=NZ election 2023 live updates: Results, analysis, reaction |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/10/nz-election-2023-live-updates-results-analysis-reaction.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231014100416/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/10/nz-election-2023-live-updates-results-analysis-reaction.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=14 October 2023 |access-date=15 October 2023 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=15 October 2023 |language=en-NZ}}</ref><ref name="2023 official results" /> Seymour retained his [[Epsom (New Zealand electorate)|Epsom]] seat and Deputy Leader [[Brooke van Velden]] won the [[Tāmaki (New Zealand electorate)|Tāmaki]] electorate.<ref>{{cite web |title=2023 General Election: Successful candidates |url=https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/successful-candidates.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=20 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231117114239/https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2023/successful-candidates.html |archive-date=17 November 2023 |date=3 November 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> In November 2023, ACT entered into a coalition deal with the National party to form part of the [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Shaw |first1=Richard |title=Three parties, two deals, one govt: The stress points within NZ's 'coalition of many colours' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/on-the-inside/503207/three-parties-two-deals-one-govt-the-stress-points-within-nz-s-coalition-of-many-colours |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |access-date=19 December 2023 |date=25 November 2023}}</ref>

As part of National's coalition agreement with ACT, the Government would adopt ACT's policies of restoring interest deductibility for rental properties and pet bonds. In addition to adopting National's youth crime and gang policies, the new Government would adopt ACT's policies of rewriting firearms legislation. The new Government would also scrap the previous Labour Government's fair pay agreements, proposed hate speech legislation, co-governance policies, Auckland light rail, [[Three Waters reform programme]], and [[Māori Health Authority]]. The Government would also establish a new [[Ministry for Regulation]] headed by Seymour that would review the quality of new and existing legislation.<ref name=newshub-election>{{cite news|last=Quinlivan |first=Mark|title= Election 2023: National, ACT and NZ First's Coalition agreement |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/11/election-2023-national-act-and-nz-first-s-coalition-agreement.html |access-date=27 November 2023|work=[[Newshub]] |date=24 November 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231124040207/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/11/election-2023-national-act-and-nz-first-s-coalition-agreement.html|archive-date=24 November 2023|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="1 News new govt">{{cite news |title=Live: Luxon makes call to Gov-General to say he can form a govt |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/11/24/live-new-govt-to-shake-up-gun-laws-co-governance-tax-rules/ |access-date=24 November 2023 |work=[[1News]] |publisher=[[TVNZ]] |date=24 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231124023819/https://www.1news.co.nz/2023/11/24/live-new-govt-to-shake-up-gun-laws-co-governance-tax-rules/ |archive-date=24 November 2023}}</ref><ref name="RNZ 24 Nov 2023">{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Coalition details at a glance: What you need to know |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/503153/coalition-details-at-a-glance-what-you-need-to-know |access-date=24 November 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=24 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231123222232/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/503153/coalition-details-at-a-glance-what-you-need-to-know |archive-date=23 November 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=newsroom-whichpolicies>{{cite news|last=Daalder|first=Mark|title=Which policies survived the negotiations – and which didn't|url=https://newsroom.co.nz/2023/11/24/which-policies-survived-the-negotiations-and-which-didnt/ |access-date=27 November 2023|work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]] |date=24 November 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231124093235/https://newsroom.co.nz/2023/11/24/which-policies-survived-the-negotiations-and-which-didnt/|archive-date=24 November 2023|url-status=live}}</ref> While National did not support Act's proposed referendum on the [[principles of the Treaty of Waitangi]], the Government would introduce a Treaty Principles Act and amend existing [[Treaty of Waitangi]] legislation to focus on the "original intent of the legislation."<ref name="RNZ 24 Nov 2023" /><ref name="RNZ Treaty legislation">{{cite news |title=New government plans to review Treaty of Waitangi principles |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/503168/new-government-plans-to-review-treaty-of-waitangi-principles |access-date=24 November 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=24 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231124032344/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/national/503168/new-government-plans-to-review-treaty-of-waitangi-principles |archive-date=24 November 2023}}</ref>

Within the National-led coalition government, Seymour became the first [[Minister for Regulation]] and was designated as [[Deputy Prime Minister of New Zealand|Deputy Prime Minister]] from 31 May 2025. Van Velden became [[Minister of Internal Affairs (New Zealand)|Minister of Internal Affairs]] and Minister for Workplace Relations and Safety. [[Nicole McKee]] became Minister for Courts and Associate Minister of Justice (firearms). [[Andrew Hoggard]] was appointed as Minister for Biosecurity and Food Safety while Karen Chhour was appointed as [[Minister for Children (New Zealand)|Minister for Children]] and Minister for the Prevention of Family and Sexual Violence. [[Simon Court]] was appointed as Parliamentary Under-Secretary for the Minister for Infrastructure and [[Resource Management Act 1991|RMA Reform]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Palmer |first1=Russell |title=Cabinet lineup for new government unveiled – who gets what? |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/503156/cabinet-lineup-for-new-government-unveiled-who-gets-what |access-date=22 December 2023 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=24 November 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204122806/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/503156/cabinet-lineup-for-new-government-unveiled-who-gets-what |archive-date=4 December 2023|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 7 February 2024, the ACT Party launched a campaign to support its Treaty Principles Bill, aiming to "restore the meaning of the Treaty of Waitangi to what was actually written and signed in 1840." ACT also reiterated its call for a referendum on the Treaty's application if the bill progresses past the initial stages. Opposition to the bill included criticism from [[Te Pāti Māori]] co-leader [[Debbie Ngarewa-Packer]], who accused Seymour of deliberately trying to divide New Zealand, and outgoing [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] MP [[Kelvin Davis (politician)|Kelvin Davis]], who stated that his party will continue to oppose the "Pākehā Government spiders".<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT launches Treaty Principles Bill 'information campaign' |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2024/02/07/act-launches-treaty-principles-bill-information-campaign/ |access-date=9 February 2024 |work=[[1News]] |language=en}}</ref>

On 7 June 2024, [[Stuff (company)|Stuff]] journalist [[Tova O'Brien]] reported that allegations of intimidation, unfair treatment of women and a "trainwreck campaign" within the ACT party had led six staff members and volunteers to resign following the 2023 general election. In addition, the party's board was facing a vote of no confidence. Complainants likened elements of the 2023 election campaign to the ''[[Hunger Games]]'' and alleged that candidates and volunteers were bullied. In response to the allegations, the ACT party confirmed that it had conducted an independent review into its campaign. The party's president Catherine Isaac stated that the party had accepted the review's recommendations and acknowledged that political campaigns could be stressful on people, who also had to balance their lives and careers.<ref>{{cite news |last1=O'Brien |first1=Tova |title=Tears and resignations after 'train wreck' ACT Party election campaign, sources claim |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/350302543/tears-and-resignations-after-train-wreck-act-party-election-campaign-sources |access-date=10 June 2024 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=7 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240607003619/https://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/350302543/tears-and-resignations-after-train-wreck-act-party-election-campaign-sources |archive-date=7 June 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> During ACT's annual rally on 9 June, Seymour downplayed allegations of a "culture of fear" within the ACT party and reiterated the party's support for the review's recommendations. In his speech, Seymour said that the party's support had grown by 1,000 percent in the past five years and set out a 15% voter target for the 2026 general election.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hanly |first1=Lillian |title=ACT leader David Seymour brushes off claims party has a 'culture of fear' |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/519090/act-leader-david-seymour-brushes-off-claims-party-has-a-culture-of-fear |access-date=10 June 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=10 June 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240609180327/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/519090/act-leader-david-seymour-brushes-off-claims-party-has-a-culture-of-fear |archive-date=9 June 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

In early July 2024, ACT invoked the "agree to disagree" provision of its coalition agreement after the National-led government decided to progress the [[Fair Digital News Bargaining Bill]], which had been introduced by the previous Labour Government.<ref>{{cite news |title=Coalition to press ahead with Fair Digital News Bargaining Bill |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/521044/coalition-to-press-ahead-with-fair-digital-news-bargaining-bill |access-date=4 July 2024 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=2 July 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240702232027/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/521044/coalition-to-press-ahead-with-fair-digital-news-bargaining-bill |archive-date=2 July 2024|url-status=live}}</ref> In late February 2025, the Government agreed to support the ACT party's proposed legislation to extend the parliamentary term from three to four years.<ref>{{cite news |title=Four-year parliamentary term legislation to be introduced, would go to referendum |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/543151/four-year-parliamentary-term-legislation-to-be-introduced-would-go-to-referendum |access-date=26 February 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=27 February 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250302025608/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/543151/four-year-parliamentary-term-legislation-to-be-introduced-would-go-to-referendum|archive-date=2 March 2025}}</ref> In mid March 2025, Seymour confirmed that ACT would be fielding candidates during the [[2025 New Zealand local elections]], marking the first time the party would contest local body elections.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ensor |first1=Jamie |title=Act Party looking to stand candidates in local elections for first time |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/act-party-looking-to-stand-candidates-in-local-elections-for-first-time/2MPL6QOKQ5AQVDT24HNAZRZDJA/ |access-date=29 March 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=18 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250319023438/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/act-party-looking-to-stand-candidates-in-local-elections-for-first-time/2MPL6QOKQ5AQVDT24HNAZRZDJA/ |archive-date=19 March 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 6 May 2025 ACT declined to support the National Party's proposed legislation forcing social media platforms to restrict access to users under the age of 16 years. Consequently, National was unable to introduce the legislation as a government bill and had to introduce it as a member's bill.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Social media ban: Act blocks National’s bid for Govt consensus on member’s bill |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/social-media-ban-act-blocks-nationals-bid-for-govt-consensus-on-members-bill/QADXWQUT3JCHDHFPDD4WXUMBOI/ |access-date=7 May 2025 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=6 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250507014200/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/social-media-ban-act-blocks-nationals-bid-for-govt-consensus-on-members-bill/QADXWQUT3JCHDHFPDD4WXUMBOI/ |archive-date=7 May 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 11 May, ACT invoked the "agree to disagree" clause of its coalition agreement after the National Party declined to support McKee's call for a review of the New Zealand government's firearms registry, and that a more "thorough and independent" review be conducted for the 2025-2026 financial year.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT invokes 'agree to disagree' clause over firearms registry review |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/560619/act-invokes-agree-to-disagree-clause-over-firearms-registry-review |access-date=13 May 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=11 May 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250513003545/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/560619/act-invokes-agree-to-disagree-clause-over-firearms-registry-review |archive-date=13 May 2025 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On 31 May 2025, [[David Seymour]] succeeded [[Winston Peters]] as deputy prime minister, under the terms of the coalition agreement in 2023.<ref>{{Cite news  |last1=McCulloch |first1=Craig |date=20 May 2025 |title=Deputy PM handover: Seymour vows straight talk, Peters fires up campaign |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/562595/deputy-pm-handover-seymour-vows-straight-talk-peters-fires-up-campaign |access-date=30 May 2025 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250530031631/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/562595/deputy-pm-handover-seymour-vows-straight-talk-peters-fires-up-campaign|archive-date=30 May 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> He became the first ACT deputy prime minister.

In June 2025, the party announced their first candidates for the [[2025 New Zealand local elections|2025 local elections]], who campaigned under the [[ACT Local]] banner. Logistics manager James Ebbett would run for the [[Central Hawke's Bay District Council]],<ref>{{cite web |author1=[[ACT New Zealand]] |title=Local Transport Specialist, James Ebbett, Selected As ACT Local Candidate For Central Hawke’s Bay Election |url=https://auckland.scoop.co.nz/2025/06/local-transport-specialist-james-ebbett-selected-as-act-local-candidate-for-central-hawkes-bay-election/ |website=auckland.scoop.co.nz |publisher=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]] |access-date=24 June 2025 |language=en |date=23 June 2024}}</ref> retired pharmacist David Ross for the [[Tasman District Council]],<ref>{{Cite press release |author=<!-- not stated --> |publisher=ACT New Zealand |date=24 June 2025 |title=Retired Pharmacist And Business Owner, David Ross, Selected As ACT Local Candidate For Motueka Ward |url= https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA2506/S00198/retired-pharmacist-and-business-owner-david-ross-selected-as-act-local-candidate-for-motueka-ward.htm |agency=[[Scoop (website)|Scoop]]}}</ref> and local businessman Nathan Atkins for the [[Waimakariri District Council]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 June 2025 |title=Business Owner Nathan Atkins To Stand With ACT Local In Kaiapoi-Woodend Ward |url=https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA2506/S00191/business-owner-nathan-atkins-to-stand-with-act-local-in-kaiapoi-woodend-ward.htm |website=Scoop}}</ref>

In late January 2026, ACT opposed the introduction of new legislation targeting [[modern slavery]]. National and Labour subsequently announced they would co-sponsor a new bill targeting modern slavery.<ref>{{cite news |title=Watch: National and Labour MPs team up to get slavery bill heard after ACT objects |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/585319/watch-national-and-labour-mps-team-up-to-get-slavery-bill-heard-after-act-objects |access-date=30 January 2026 |work=[[RNZ]] |date=29 January 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260129061204/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/585319/watch-national-and-labour-mps-team-up-to-get-slavery-bill-heard-after-act-objects |archive-date=29 January 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

In late June 2026, the associate justice minister [[Nicole McKee]] was appointed as ACT's deputy leader during the party's annual general meeting in Auckland.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT reveals its new deputy leader: 'My whakapapa is mine' |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2026/06/28/act-reveals-its-new-deputy-leader-my-whakapapa-is-mine/ |access-date=28 June 2026 |work=[[1News]] |date=28 June 2026}}</ref>

== Principles ==
{{Liberalism New Zealand|Parties}}
According to former party leader [[Rodney Hide]], ACT's values are "individual freedom, personal responsibility, doing the best for our natural environment and for smaller, smarter government in its goals of a prosperous economy, a strong society, and a quality of life that is the envy of the world".<ref name="Rodney Hide">[http://www.act.org.nz/news-article.aspx?id=27624 Rodney Hide] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060920224737/http://www.act.org.nz/news-article.aspx?id=27624|date=20 September 2006}}, "Speech to ACT Auckland Regional Conference, 30 July 2006"</ref> ACT states that it adheres to [[classical-liberal]] and [[small government|small]] (or [[Limited government|limited]]) government principles coupled with what the party considers as a high regard for [[Political individualism|individual freedom]] and [[personal responsibility]].<ref>[http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA0805/S00321.htm ACT's Pledge To New Zealand], reported on 19 May 2008</ref><ref name="Seymour2" /> ACT sets out its values:
* The principal object of the Party is to promote an open and benevolent society in which individual New Zealanders are free to achieve their full potential.<ref>{{cite web |title=ACT Party |url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/political-parties/act-party/ |website=NZ Parliament}}</ref>
* That individuals are the rightful owners of their own [[personal life|lives]] and therefore have inherent [[Freedom (political)|freedom]]s and responsibilities.
* That the proper purpose of government is to protect such freedoms and not to assume such responsibilities.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.act.org.nz/policy_ataglance.aspx|date=5 January 2007|title=ACT Policy at a Glance|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070105163327/http://www.act.org.nz/policy_ataglance.aspx|archive-date=5 January 2007}}</ref>
* All people should be equal before the law regardless of race, gender, sexuality, religion or political belief.<ref name="act.org.nz">{{Cite web|url=https://www.act.org.nz/principles|title=Principles|website=ACT|access-date=30 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240220134957/https://www.act.org.nz/principles|archive-date=20 February 2024|url-status=dead}}</ref>
* [[Freedom of expression]] is essential to a free society and must be promoted, protected and preserved without restriction other than for incitement, criminal nuisance or defamation.<ref name="act.org.nz"/>
* Citizenship and permanent residency should be subject to applicants affirming New Zealand's values.<ref name="act.org.nz"/>

==Policies and ideology==
[[File:ACT New Zealand 2003 logo.gif|thumb|upright|left|ACT has described itself as 'The Liberal Party'.]]
ACT has been characterised as a [[conservative]],<ref name="Washington Examiner">{{cite news |last= Perry |first= Nick |date= 14 August 2014 |title= Racism mars New Zealand election campaign |url= https://www.washingtonexaminer.com/racism-mars-new-zealand-election-campaign |work= Washington Examiner |access-date= 13 December 2020}}</ref><ref name="News AU">{{cite news |date= 27 November 2011 |title= NZ votes for stability after chaos |url= https://www.news.com.au/world/nz-votes-for-stability-after-chaos/news-story/eed3c5c5e1686793119b3807b15438db |work= News.com.au |access-date= 13 December 2020}}</ref><ref name="The Economist">{{cite news |date= 18 October 2014 |title= Third term lucky |url= https://www.economist.com/banyan/2014/10/18/third-term-lucky |newspaper=[[The Economist]] |access-date= 13 December 2020}}</ref> [[classical liberal]] and [[libertarian]] party.<ref name="Norris2005"/><ref name="policy.nz">{{cite web|title=Party profile: ACT Party — NZ Election 2020|url=https://policy.nz/parties/ACT-Party|access-date=2020-09-25|website=Your complete guide to NZ Election 2020 – Policy|quote=The ACT Party (Rōpū ACT) is a right-wing libertarian party that advocates free market policies and reducing the role of government.|archive-date=18 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201018073035/https://policy.nz/parties/ACT-Party|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="Boston2003"/> ACT's platform featured conservative [[populist]] policies under former leaders [[Richard Prebble]] (in office: 1996–2004) and [[Rodney Hide]] (2004–2011).<ref>{{cite news|title=ACT's battle for significance|date=4 October 2017|work=[[Newsroom (website)|Newsroom]]|url= https://www.newsroom.co.nz/2017/10/03/51394/acts-battle-for-significance#}}</ref> Under the leadership of [[David Seymour]] (in office from 2014 onwards), commentators have identified a shift in policy to a more libertarian outlook.<ref name="Graham-McLay-2020"/><ref name="Duncan-2020" /> Although Seymour has stated that he does not view [[populism]] as the way to govern a country or to stimulate growth,<ref>{{Cite web|url= https://www.act.org.nz/populism_is_not_a_plan_we_need_a_roadmap_to_recovery|title= Populism is not a plan – we need a roadmap to recovery|website= ACT|access-date= 27 January 2021|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20240521235004/https://www.act.org.nz/populism_is_not_a_plan_we_need_a_roadmap_to_recovery|archive-date= 21 May 2024|url-status= dead}}</ref> market populism remained a prominent part of his and the ACT party's 2023 election campaign.<ref>{{cite chapter |last1=Oldfield |first1=Luke |editor1-last=Levine |editor1-first=Stephen |title=Back on Track? The New Zealand General Election of 2023 |date=2024 |publisher=Victoria University Press |location=Wellington |isbn=9781776922178 |page=264 |url=https://openaccess.wgtn.ac.nz/ndownloader/files/49987113 |chapter=Populists, Populism and the 2023 Election}}</ref> The ACT Party emphasises the importance of [[Property rights (economics)|property rights]].<ref>
{{cite press release
| date             = 14 June 2022
| title            = ACT the only party to support property rights
| url              = https://www.act.org.nz/act-the-only-party-to-support-property-rights
| publisher        = ACT New Zealand
| access-date      = 27 November 2023
| quote            = 'ACT is the only party in Parliament who truly supports property rights [...],' says ACT Leader David Seymour.
}}
</ref><ref>
{{cite web
| url          = https://www.act.org.nz/policies
| title        = ACT's policies
| year         = 2022
| publisher    = ACT New Zealand
| access-date  = 27 November 2023
| quote        = ACT believes protecting the safety and property of its citizens is the government's first and most important job.
}}
</ref>

ACT wants to reduce or remove some government programmes which it sees as unnecessary and wasteful, and to increase self-reliance by encouraging individuals to take responsibility to pay for services traditionally paid for by governments. Under leader Rodney Hide, ACT New Zealand had primarily focused on two main policy areas: taxation and crime ([[Law and order (politics)|law-and-order issues]]). At the [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011 general election]], ACT advocated lowering tax rates and also supported something approaching a [[flat tax]], in which tax rates would not be graduated based on wealth or income, so that every taxpayer would pay the same proportion of their income in tax. The flat tax-rate that ACT proposed was approximately 15%, with no tax on the first $25,000 for those who opt out of state-provided accident, sickness and healthcare cover.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.act.org.nz/taxation-policy|title=ACT Taxation Policy |access-date=25 July 2009|url-status=dead|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120222055801/http://www.act.org.nz/taxation-policy|archive-date=22 February 2012}}</ref> {{As of|2021|alt=As at 2021}}, the party proposed reducing [[Goods and Services Tax (New Zealand)|GST]] and decreasing the [[marginal tax rate]] paid by those on the median wage, but did not advocate a flat tax rate.<ref>{{cite web |title=A Middle Income Tax Cut |url=https://www.act.org.nz/middle_income_tax_cut |publisher= ACT |access-date=10 January 2021 |language=en |archive-date=19 January 2021 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210119203421/https://www.act.org.nz/middle_income_tax_cut |url-status=dead}}</ref>

During the [[2020 New Zealand election|2020 general election]], the ACT party campaigned on a broad policy platform. It prioritised economic recovery (''see: {{slink|COVID-19 pandemic in New Zealand|Long-term effects}}''), keeping national debt low, and signing up to a [[CANZUK]] agreement which would enable free movement of people and goods between the United Kingdom, New Zealand, Canada and Australia. The party wants to protect freedom of expression and to limit funding for universities that do not uphold freedom of speech on campus. It supports immigration while it calls for compulsory measures for immigrants to assimilate and for limiting citizenship or permanent-residency status to those who pledge to uphold the values of New Zealand.<ref>{{Cite web|url= https://www.act.org.nz/policies |title= Policies|publisher=ACT|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201016145654/https://www.act.org.nz/policies |archive-date=16 October 2020|year=2020}}</ref>

===Law and order===
On its website, ACT states that rights should not depend on ancestry, and that they stand for "one law for all".<ref>{{cite web |title=Equal Rights & Democracy |url=https://www.act.org.nz/policies/democracy |website=www.act.org.nz |publisher=ACT New Zealand |access-date=20 October 2026 |language=en-NZ}}</ref> The party has a number of tough-on-crime policies focused primarily on trying to control [[Gangs in New Zealand|gangs]]. Party leader, David Seymour, has supported the "[[Sentencing and Parole Reform Act 2010|three strikes law]]," reimposing it after its repeal in 2022.<ref>{{cite web |title= Law and Order |url=https://www.act.org.nz/law-and-order |publisher=ACT Party |access-date=13 May 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230124184840/https://www.act.org.nz/policies/law-and-order |archive-date=24 January 2023 |url-status=live}}</ref> ACT advocates repealing [[Arms (Prohibited Firearms, Magazines, and Parts) Amendment Act 2019|New Zealand's 2019 firearms legislation]], and taking a "tougher" stance on criminals who repeatedly offend and on those found guilty of violent crimes, while also supporting rehabilitation programs.<ref>{{cite press release |last1=Seymour |first1=David |title=PM out of touch on crime and guns: ACT Party |url=https://www.act.org.nz/press-releases/pm-out-of-touch-on-crime-and-guns |website=act.org.nz |publisher=ACT New Zealand |access-date=7 February 2023 |archive-date=6 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230206183447/https://www.act.org.nz/press-releases/pm-out-of-touch-on-crime-and-guns |url-status=dead }}</ref>

In September 2022, the ACT Party proposed fitting [[electronic tagging|ankle bracelet]]s on young offenders aged between 11 and 14 to combat juvenile crime, particularly [[ram-raiding]]. Party leader Seymour argued that ankle bracelets were non-intrusive and would allow police to monitor young offenders.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Manch |first1=Thomas |title=ACT Party wants to put ankle bracelets on youth offenders who conduct ramraids |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/129800473/act-party-wants-to-put-ankle-bracelets-on-youth-offenders-who-conduct-ramraids |access-date= 6 January 2023 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=6 September 2022|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20221103052113/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/129800473/act-party-wants-to-put-ankle-bracelets-on-youth-offenders-who-conduct-ramraids|archive-date=3 November 2022|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Newshub 8 Sep 2022">{{cite news |last1=Hewett |first1=William |title=David Seymour stands by ACT's idea to put young offenders who commit serious crimes in ankle bracelets amid criticism |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/09/david-seymour-stands-by-act-s-idea-to-put-young-offenders-who-commit-serious-crimes-in-ankle-bracelets-amid-criticism.html |access-date= 6 January 2023 |work= [[Newshub]] |date=8 September 2022|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20220918144634/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/09/david-seymour-stands-by-act-s-idea-to-put-young-offenders-who-commit-serious-crimes-in-ankle-bracelets-amid-criticism.html|archive-date=18 September 2022}}</ref> In response, Police Minister [[Chris Hipkins]] stated that the Labour Government was not considering the use of ankle bracelets for young offenders but would keep "all options on the table".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Quinlivan |first1=Mark |title=Idea of putting youth offenders in ankle bracelets 'breaks my heart', National MP Erica Stanford says |url= https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/10/idea-of-putting-youth-offenders-in-ankle-bracelets-breaks-my-heart-national-mp-erica-stanford-says.html |access-date= 6 January 2023 |work= [[Newshub]] |date= 21 October 2022|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20221118205304/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/10/idea-of-putting-youth-offenders-in-ankle-bracelets-breaks-my-heart-national-mp-erica-stanford-says.html|archive-date=18 November 2022|url-status= dead | quote = On Thursday, Police Minister Chris Hipkins indicated in Parliament putting ankle bracelets on ram raiders wasn't off the table. [...] 'In terms of the ankle bracelet policy, the issue of removing some of the repeat offenders from circulation and keeping them out of circulation is something the Government is very focused on,' said Hipkins, under questioning from ACT Police spokesperson Chris Baillie. 'I'm not convinced that extra use of ankle bracelets would necessarily achieve that but we're keeping all options on the table.'}}</ref> National Party leader [[Christopher Luxon]] and education spokesperson [[Erica Stanford]] initially indicated that they would not support ACT's ankle-bracelet policy, with Stanford describing it as "heartbreaking".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Quinlivan |first1=Mark |title=Idea of putting youth offenders in ankle bracelets 'breaks my heart', National MP Erica Stanford says |url= https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/10/idea-of-putting-youth-offenders-in-ankle-bracelets-breaks-my-heart-national-mp-erica-stanford-says.html |access-date= 6 January 2023 |work= [[Newshub]] |date= 21 October 2022|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20221118205304/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/10/idea-of-putting-youth-offenders-in-ankle-bracelets-breaks-my-heart-national-mp-erica-stanford-says.html|archive-date=18 November 2022|url-status= dead}}</ref> New Zealand rugby-league player Sir [[Graham Lowe]] criticised the ankle-bracelet policy, while [[Waikato]] retailer Ash Parmer supported it.<ref name="Newshub 8 Sep 2022" /> In November 2022, National reversed its initial opposition to fitting young offenders with ankle bracelets; with justice spokesperson [[Paul Goldsmith (politician)|Paul Goldsmith]] stating that a law change was necessary to impose electronic monitoring or intensive supervision on child offenders aged under 12 years.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Pearse |first1=Adam |title=Law change needed to facilitate National's plan to put ankle bracelets on 10-year-olds |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/law-change-needed-to-facilitate-nationals-plan-to-put-ankle-bracelets-on-10-year-olds/Y7AYZQCJVNCWDC5I6XUVGFIPOU/ |access-date=6 January 2023 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=18 November 2022 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20221128045350/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/politics/law-change-needed-to-facilitate-nationals-plan-to-put-ankle-bracelets-on-10-year-olds/Y7AYZQCJVNCWDC5I6XUVGFIPOU/ |archive-date=28 November 2022|url-status=live}}</ref>

In May 2023, Seymour announced that ACT, if elected into government, would build youth-detention centres run by the [[Department of Corrections (New Zealand)|Department of Corrections]]. ACT's proposed policies include shifting management of youth offenders from [[Oranga Tamariki]] (the Ministry for Children) to the Corrections Department, investing NZ$677&nbsp;million into combating youth crime over the following four years – including NZ$500&nbsp;million on the construction of 200 new "youth justice beds", and $44&nbsp;million per year to operate these facilities.<ref>{{cite web |last1= Seymour |first1= David |title= Holding youth offenders accountable |url=https://www.act.org.nz/holding_young_offenders_accountable |publisher=ACT Party |access-date=13 May 2023 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230508003556/https://www.act.org.nz/holding_young_offenders_accountable |archive-date= 8 May 2023 |date= 8 May 2023 |url-status= live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1= Hewett |first1= William |title= How ACT wants to end 'tag-and-release game' of juvenile offending  |url= https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/05/how-act-wants-to-end-tag-and-release-game-of-juvenile-offending.html |access-date= 13 May 2023 |work= [[Newshub]] |date=10 May 2023 |ref= live |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230510040133/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/05/how-act-wants-to-end-tag-and-release-game-of-juvenile-offending.html |archive-date=10 May 2023|url-status=dead}}</ref>

===Social issues===
Members of ACT's [[caucus]] in parliament voted five to four in favour of the [[Civil Union Act 2004]] which gave the option of legal recognition to (among others) same-sex couples. A majority within the caucus also supported the legalisation of brothels by the [[Prostitution Reform Act 2003]].<ref name="prostitution-reform">{{cite news |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=3509424 |title= Barnett celebrates 'historic moment' with prostitution bill |access-date= 27 October 2008 |last= Taylor | first= Kevin |agency= NZPA |date= 26 June 2003|work= [[The New Zealand Herald]]}}</ref> In 2005, both of ACT's MPs, [[Rodney Hide]] and [[Heather Roy]], voted for the [[Same-sex marriage in New Zealand|Marriage (Gender Clarification) Amendment Bill 2005]], which would have banned the possibility of introducing [[same-sex marriage]] in New Zealand in the future perspective.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/pb/hansard-debates/rhr/document/48HansD_20051207_00001276/marriage-gender-clarification-amendment-bill-first|title= Marriage (Gender Clarification) Amendment Bill — First Reading – New Zealand Parliament|website=parliament.nz|date= 5 March 2024}}</ref>

In 2013, leader [[John Banks (New Zealand politician)|John Banks]] (the party's sole MP from 2011 to 2014) voted in favour of the [[Marriage (Definition of Marriage) Amendment Bill]] at its third reading, a law which legalised [[same-sex marriage in New Zealand]].<ref>{{cite news |title= Marriage equality bill : How MPs voted |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/8454794/Marriage-equality-bill-How-MPs-voted |access-date= 17 December 2020 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date= 2 April 2013 |language=en}}</ref>

ACT leader David Seymour supported the legalisation of [[Euthanasia in New Zealand|assisted dying]]. In 2018, he introduced a [[Private member's bill|member's bill]], the [[End of Life Choice Bill]], which aimed to legalise [[euthanasia in New Zealand]].<ref>{{cite press release |last1= Seymour|first1= David |author-link1= David Seymour |title= Seymour lodges assisted dying Bill|url= http://www.act.org.nz/posts/seymour-lodges-assisted-dying-bill |publisher= ACT New Zealand |access-date=4 December 2015|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20151208154237/http://www.act.org.nz/posts/seymour-lodges-assisted-dying-bill|archive-date= 8 December 2015|url-status= dead}}</ref> The law passed in 2019, was approved by the public in [[2020 New Zealand euthanasia referendum|a 2020 referendum]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Election 2020: 'What a great day to be a Kiwi' — David Seymour celebrates euthanasia referendum passing |url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300146022/election-2020-what-a-great-day-to-be-a-kiwi--david-seymour-celebrates-euthanasia-referendum-passing |access-date=17 December 2020 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=30 October 2020 |language=en}}</ref> and took full effect in 2021. Grant Duncan cited the euthanasia law as an example of ACT neoliberalism.<ref name="Duncan-2020"/> In 2020, Seymour voted for the [[Abortion Legislation Act 2020|Abortion Legislation Act]] which introduced abortion on request.<ref>{{Cite web|url= https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA2003/S00138/act-welcomes-abortion-legislation-act.htm|title=ACT Welcomes Abortion Legislation Act &#124; Scoop News|website= scoop.co.nz}}</ref> However, he criticised a particular aspect of this law which created "free protest zones" which would ban protests near abortion clinics, saying this limits freedom of expression.<ref>{{Cite web|url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/120251127/acts-david-seymour-the-abortion-lobbyists-and-the-facebook-scrap|title= ACT's David Seymour, the abortion lobbyists, and the Facebook scrap|date=13 March 2020|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] }}</ref> 
In 2021, ACT expressed support for liberalisation of [[Surrogacy in New Zealand|surrogacy law]] so as to facilitate availability of surrogate services to heterosexual and [[same-sex relationship|same-sex]] couples as well.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT backing Tamati Coffey's surrogacy bill |url= https://www.tvnz.co.nz/one-news/new-zealand/act-backing-tamati-coffeys-surrogacy-bill |access-date= 11 October 2021 |work= 1 News |publisher= TVNZ |date=14 July 2021 |language=en-NZ}}</ref> (Currently, New Zealand law permits [[surrogacy|altruistic surrogacy]] only.)

===Māori issues===
ACT proposes abolition of the [[Māori electorate]] seats in the New Zealand Parliament, arguing the seats are "an anachronism and offensive to the principle of equal citizenship" and that Māori MPs have been elected in general elections on other lists without special assistance. The party also wants to reduce the number of MPs in parliament from 120 to 100.<ref>{{Cite web|url= https://www.act.org.nz/a_more_democratic_country|title=A More Democratic New Zealand |website= act.org.nz|access-date=23 June 2022|archive-date=23 June 2022 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20220623083826/https://www.act.org.nz/a_more_democratic_country|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In March 2022, ACT campaigned on holding a referendum on [[Māori people|Māori]] [[co-governance]] arrangements as a condition for entering into coalition with the National Party. Seymour has argued that the 1840 [[Treaty of Waitangi]] was not a partnership between the [[New Zealand Crown]] and Māori, and that co-governance arrangements created resentment and division. In addition, ACT announced that it would introduce a new law defining the [[principles of the Treaty of Waitangi]] if elected into government following the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 election]]. This law would only come into effect following a referendum held at the 2026 general election.<ref name="TVNZ co-governance">{{cite news |last1=Sherman |first1=Maiki |title=ACT Party wants referendum on co-governance with Māori |url= https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/03/24/act-party-wants-referendum-on-co-governance-with-maori/ |access-date=27 January 2023 |work=[[1News]] |publisher=[[TVNZ]] |date= 24 March 2022|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230127031536/https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/03/24/act-party-wants-referendum-on-co-governance-with-maori/|archive-date=27 January 2023 |url-status= live}}</ref> ACT's proposed referendum and law would affect co-governance arrangements at several [[Crown Research Institutes]], [[state-owned enterprise]]s and healthcare providers such as [[Te Aka Whai Ora]] (the Māori Health Authority). However, Seymour indicated that the new law would preserve existing co-governance arrangements with the [[Waikato]], [[Ngāi Tahu]], [[Tūhoe]] and [[Whanganui]] [[iwi]] (tribes).<ref name="Newshub Te Tiriti">{{cite news |last1= Forbes |first1= Mihirangi Forbes |title= Act Party – rewrites Te Tiriti interpretation |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/03/act-s-david-seymour-explains-why-he-wants-m-ori-co-governance-referendum.html |access-date= 27 January 2023 |work= [[Newshub]] |date=29 March 2022|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230127031533/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/03/act-s-david-seymour-explains-why-he-wants-m-ori-co-governance-referendum.html|archive-date=27 January 2023|url-status=dead}}</ref>

[[Māori Party]] co-leader [[Debbie Ngarewa-Packer]] and Professor [[Linda Tuhiwai Smith]] described ACT's proposed co-governance referendum and policies as being motivated by racism and reflecting a [[Pākehā]] unwillingness to share power. Similarly, Waikato leader Rahui Papa claimed that ACT's co-governance policies clashed with the second and third articles of the treaty which (he argued) guaranteed Māori participation in the social sector.<ref name="Newshub Te Tiriti" /><ref name="TVNZ co-governance" /> In response, Prime Minister [[Jacinda Ardern]] reiterated [[Sixth Labour Government of New Zealand|her government]]'s commitment to co-governance arrangements. Meanwhile, National Party leader [[Christopher Luxon]] refused to commit to a referendum on co-governance, but acknowledged that further clarity on co-governance was needed.<ref name="TVNZ co-governance" />

In October 2022, ACT released a discussion document entitled "Democracy or co-government?" which proposed a new Treaty Principles Act that would end the focus on partnership between Māori and the Crown and interpret "[[tino rangatiratanga]]" solely as property rights. By contrast, most scholars of the [[Māori language]] define "tino rangatiratanga" as the equivalent of "[[self-determination]]" in the English language. The proposed Treaty Principles Act does not mention Māori, the Crown, [[iwi]] (tribes), and [[hapū]] (subgroups) but refers only to "New Zealanders".  ACT Party leader Seymour refused to identify whom his party had consulted when developing its co-governance and Treaty of Waitangi policies, particularly its redefinition of "tino rangatiratanga" as property rights. As part of ACT's non-racial (colour-blind) policies, its social-development spokesperson [[Karen Chhour]] advocated the abolition of Te Aka Whai Ora.<ref>{{cite news |last1= McConnell |first1=Glenn |title=ACT increases 'co-governance' focus, says Treaty referendum a 'bottom line' |url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/130122915/act-increases-cogovernance-focus-says-treaty-referendum-a-bottom-line |access-date=28 January 2023 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=10 October 2022|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20230114091548/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/130122915/act-increases-cogovernance-focus-says-treaty-referendum-a-bottom-line |archive-date=14 January 2023|url-status=live}}</ref>

Following the 2023 election and the formation of a [[Sixth National Government of New Zealand|National-led coalition government]], ACT embarked on a public information campaign in early February 2024 to promote its [[Treaty Principles Bill]]. This campaign includes the creation of a new website called "treaty.nz," which has a Questions and Answers section outlining the party's approach to the principles of the Treaty of Waitangi and a video featuring Seymour. Seymour also contested claims that the opposition was trying to rewrite or abolish the Treaty of Waitangi. The public information campaign also came after a leaked [[Ministry of Justice (New Zealand)|Justice Ministry]] memo claimed that the proposed bill clashed with the text of the Treaty.<ref>{{cite news |title=ACT launches Treaty Principles Bill information campaign |url=https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/508579/act-launches-treaty-principles-bill-information-campaign |access-date=7 February 2024 |work=[[Radio New Zealand]] |date=7 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240207015928/https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/508579/act-launches-treaty-principles-bill-information-campaign |archive-date=7 February 2024|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Climate change===

ACT went into the 2008 general election with a policy that in part stated "New Zealand is not warming" and that their policy goal was to ensure "That no New Zealand government will ever impose needless and unjustified taxation or regulation on its citizens in a misguided attempt to reduce [[global warming]] or become a world leader in [[carbon neutrality]]".<ref name="policy18">{{cite web|url= http://www.act.org.nz/files/plan/policy18.pdf |title=ACT Climate Change Policy |date= 6 October 2008 |url-status=dead |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20140502091242/http://www.act.org.nz/files/plan/policy18.pdf |archive-date=2 May 2014 }}</ref> In September 2008, ACT Party Leader [[Rodney Hide]] stated "that the entire [[climate change]] – global warming hypothesis is a hoax, that the data and the hypothesis do not hold together, that [[Al Gore]] is a phoney and a fraud on this issue, and that the [[emissions trading]] scheme is a worldwide scam and swindle."<ref>{{cite web|last= Hide|first= Rodney|url= http://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PA0809/S00042.htm |title=Hide: Emissions Trading Bill |work=Press Release ACT Party Speech to Parliament|publisher=scoop.co.nz|date= 3 September 2008|access-date= 12 January 2010}}</ref> The former party leader has been branded as an "outspoken Kiwi [[Climate change denial|climate change sceptic]]".<ref>{{cite news | last= Theunissen | first= Matthew | date= 27 June 2015 | title= Climate change? Yeah, nah | url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/climate-change-yeah-nah/WT2T4LBWZMTFNVNKSX7QZHSUKA/ | work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] | access-date= 13 December 2020}}</ref>  In February 2016, ACT deleted this climate-change policy from their website, and party leader David Seymour criticised the [[Green Party of Aotearoa New Zealand|Green Party]] for doing "bugger all for the environment".<ref name="ACT deletes climate change policy">{{cite news |title=Coup ACT deletes climate change policy from their website |first=Henry |last=Cooke |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/77338800/act-delete-climate-change-policy-from-their-website |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=27 February 2016 |access-date=27 February 2016}}</ref>

ACT placed [[Chris Baillie (politician)|Chris Baillie]] fourth on its [[Party lists in the 2020 New Zealand general election|party list of candidates in the 2020 election]]; he has received criticism over his views on climate change,<ref>{{cite news |last= Newman |first= Tim |date= 5 July 2020 |title = Nelson ACT candidate responds to climate change criticism 'hysteria' |url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/122026299/nelson-act-candidate-responds-to-climate-change-criticism-hysteria | work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] | access-date= 13 December 2020}}</ref> and been labelled a climate-change sceptic.<ref>{{cite news | date= 28 June 2020 | title= Vote 2020: ACT list makes gun lobbyist and climate change denier electable | url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/business/vote-2020-act-list-makes-gun-lobbyist-and-climate-change-denier-electable/SZMQPG2BNGS6HEKO3W33VZ5CCA/ | work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date= 13 December 2020}}</ref> In the runup to the 2020 election, Environmental Defence Society chief executive Gary Taylor said that even ACT had moved its position from where it had been. He was largely critical of the party, saying: "ACT have been very outspoken about wanting to go hard to repeal a lot of climate change legislation, and I haven't seen much from [[New Zealand First]], mainly just silence." He also stated: "I think the only upside from ACT really on climate change is they do seem to have moved from outright deniers – which is where the party was five years ago. [With] a strong ACT presence you could expect some of their radical and unhelpful policies to potentially be implemented, and that is frankly a scary proposition."<ref>{{cite news |last = Huffadine |first = Leith |date = 13 October 2020 |title = Politicians have a 'mandate' for climate action, how do their policies stack up? |url = https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/political/428210/politicians-have-a-mandate-for-climate-action-how-do-their-policies-stack-up |work = Radio New Zealand (RNZ) |access-date = 13 December 2020}}</ref>

In early December 2020, the New Zealand Parliament officially [[Climate emergency declaration|declared a climate emergency]], of which ACT was critical, stating, "Today's climate emergency was a triumph for post-rational politics with feelings rather than facts driving the Government's response to climate change".<ref>{{cite news |last = Walls |first = Jason |date = 2 December 2020 |title = Parliament passes climate emergency motion in House |url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/parliament-passes-climate-emergency-motion-in-house/3LQXDOR73G2SAV7LCCRKFWTNGI/ |work =[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date = 13 December 2020}}</ref> The party supports repealing the 2019 "[[Climate Change Response (Zero Carbon) Amendment Act|Zero Carbon Act]]".<ref name="Stuff climate">{{cite news | last= Wannan | first= Olivia | date= 29 September 2020 | title= ACT, NZ First policies ignore, worsen climate change – health advocates | url= https://www.stuff.co.nz/environment/climate-news/122914136/act-nz-first-policies-ignore-worsen-climate-change--health-advocates |work =[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] | access-date= 13 December 2020}}</ref>

=== Foreign policy ===
In 2019, ACT expressed sympathy with the Hong Kong pro-democracy protestors during the [[2019–20 Hong Kong protests]] with party leader David Seymour speaking at a Hong Kong pro-democracy rally at the [[University of Auckland]]. The party argued that the New Zealand government should condemn efforts by the Chinese government to restrict freedom of speech in Hong Kong and criticised the [[Chinese Consulate-General, Auckland|Chinese Consulate-General]] for praising a Chinese student who had assaulted a pro-democracy activist in Auckland.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Tan |first1=Lincoln |title=More than 100 gather for Hong Kong protest at the University of Auckland |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12256015 |access-date=7 August 2019 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=6 August 2019 |archive-date=9 August 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190809020307/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12256015 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Christian |first1=Harrison |title=Hong Kong protesters gather at Auckland University |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/auckland/114777487/hong-kong-protesters-gather-at-auckland-university |access-date=7 August 2019 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=6 August 2019 |archive-date=7 August 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190807000545/https://www.stuff.co.nz/auckland/114777487/hong-kong-protesters-gather-at-auckland-university |url-status=live }}</ref>

In response to the [[Gaza war]], Seymour as ACT leader issued a statement in support of Israel and condemned Hamas terrorism. ACT also accused Labour Foreign Minister [[Nanaia Mahuta]] of not explicitly opposing Hamas' actions.<ref>{{cite news |title=NZ leaders respond to Gaza attacks |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/live-updates/08-10-2023/nz-leaders-respond-to-gaza-attacks |access-date=10 October 2023 |work=[[The Spinoff]] |date=8 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231008042537/https://thespinoff.co.nz/live-updates/08-10-2023/nz-leaders-respond-to-gaza-attacks |archive-date=8 October 2023|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Hewett |first1=Wiliam |title=Nanaia Mahuta calls for 'immediate cessation of violence' as hundreds killed in Israel-Hamas conflict |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/10/nanaia-mahuta-calls-for-immediate-cessation-of-violence-as-hundreds-killed-in-israel-hamas-conflict.html |access-date=10 October 2023 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=8 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231010003719/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2023/10/nanaia-mahuta-calls-for-immediate-cessation-of-violence-as-hundreds-killed-in-israel-hamas-conflict.html |archive-date=10 October 2023}}</ref>

